      {
        "t": 9.1,
        "end": 10.1,
        "label": "Find zero of tangent line",
        "values": [
          "x_0 = 0.5",
          "f(x_0) = -0.375",
          "f'(x_0) = -0.25",
          "x_1 = -1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "t": 11.6,
        "end": 13.6,
        "label": "Converged to −1, not the nearest root",
        "values": [
          "x_1 = -1",
          "f(x_1) = 0",
          "\\text{—}",
          "\\text{—}"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "discussion": {
      "title": "Newton's Method",
      "subtitle": "Pitfalls: Converging to the Wrong Root",
      "text": "Newton's method converges to a root, not necessarily the closest one. Starting at $x_0 = 0.5$ on $f(x) = x^3 - x$, the tangent is shallow ($f'(x_0) = -0.25$), so the step $-\\frac{f(x_0)}{f'(x_0)} = -1.5$ throws the guess past $0$ straight to $x_1 = -1$. A flat tangent means a big jump, and a big jump can land in another root's basin."
    }
  },
