      {
        "t": 6.0,
        "end": 8.0,
        "label": "Apply the update rule",
        "values": [
          "x_0 = 0",
          "f(x_0) = -1",
          "f'(x_0) = 0",
          "x_1 = 0 - \\frac{-1}{0}"
        ]
      },
      {
        "t": 9.5,
        "end": 11.5,
        "label": "Division by zero: no x₁",
        "values": [
          "x_0 = 0",
          "f(x_0) = -1",
          "f'(x_0) = 0",
          "\\text{undefined}"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "discussion": {
      "title": "Newton's Method",
      "subtitle": "Pitfalls: Zero Slope",
      "text": "The update rule divides by $f'(x_n)$. At $x_0 = 0$ on $f(x) = x^2 - 1$, $f'(x_0) = 0$, so the tangent is horizontal and never meets the x-axis. $x_1$ is undefined, and guesses near a stationary point have the same problem in a milder form: a tiny slope sends $x_1$ very far away."
    }
  },
